# Conservation Renewal Agenda

## v0.4.5 — Wildlife & Biodiversity Case-Study Evidence Matrix

### 1. Purpose

This matrix is the first structured comparison of species-level conservation cases within the Wildlife & Biodiversity pillar.

The objective is not to determine whether the Endangered Species Act or any individual conservation program is simply “successful” or “unsuccessful.” Instead, the cases are used to examine how the broader conservation system converts institutional capacity, scientific information, funding, management actions, and coordination into measurable biological outcomes.

The analysis therefore treats the ESA as one component of a larger conservation system.

The central analytical question is:

> **Under what conditions does the American conservation system successfully move a species from imperilment toward stabilization, recovery, and durable persistence—and where does that process break down?**

---

# 2. Case-Study Design

The initial analytical sample consists of approximately 24 species divided into four outcome groups.

| Outcome group                            | Purpose                                                                                                              | Initial cases                                                                                                                      |
| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Recovery-based delisting                 | Examine cases where conservation resulted in formal recovery and removal from the ESA list                           | Cumberland sandwort; San Benito evening-primrose; Running buffalo clover; water howellia; Bradshaw's lomatium; interior least tern |
| Downlisting / substantial improvement    | Examine cases showing substantial biological or conservation improvement without full recovery                       | June sucker; Stephens' kangaroo rat; Morro shoulderband snail; smooth coneflower; humpback chub; American burying beetle           |
| Persistent concern / limited improvement | Examine species where long-term conservation effort has not yet produced sufficient improvement for reclassification | Rusty patched bumble bee; red wolf; San Joaquin kit fox; pallid sturgeon; dusky gopher frog; California condor                     |
| Deterioration / increased concern        | Examine cases where biological conditions worsened or threats increased despite existing conservation institutions   | Northern long-eared bat and additional cases to be selected following review of five-year-status-review evidence                   |

The fourth category is intentionally incomplete at this stage. Additional deterioration cases should be selected from the larger ESA dataset rather than chosen simply because they are well-known examples.

This prevents the case-study sample from becoming anecdotal.

---

# 3. Evidence-Coding Framework

Each case will be evaluated against the following factors.

| Factor                          | Documented                                            | Partially documented                         | Not documented             | Contradictory                           | Insufficient evidence    |
| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | -------------------------- | --------------------------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Early detection/intervention    | Evidence indicates intervention before severe decline | Some evidence                                | No clear evidence          | Evidence points in different directions | Insufficient information |
| Habitat protection              | Habitat protection appears important to outcome       | Some role                                    | Little documented role     | Conflicting evidence                    | Insufficient information |
| Threat reduction                | Major threats were reduced                            | Some threats reduced                         | Major threats persisted    | Mixed                                   | Insufficient information |
| Active management               | Direct management materially involved                 | Some role                                    | Limited role               | Mixed                                   | Insufficient information |
| Monitoring                      | Monitoring informed decisions                         | Monitoring occurred but limited adaptive use | Minimal evidence           | Mixed                                   | Insufficient information |
| Recovery-plan implementation    | Major recovery actions implemented                    | Partial implementation                       | Limited implementation     | Mixed                                   | Insufficient information |
| Cross-jurisdiction coordination | Multiple governments/institutions coordinated         | Some coordination                            | Limited coordination       | Conflict/friction documented            | Insufficient information |
| Private-land participation      | Private landowners materially involved                | Some involvement                             | Limited involvement        | Conflict/constraint                     | Insufficient information |
| Long-term funding/capacity      | Sustained resources documented                        | Intermittent resources                       | Major capacity limitations | Mixed                                   | Insufficient information |
| Adaptive management             | Management changed in response to evidence            | Some adaptation                              | Little evidence            | Conflicting evidence                    | Insufficient information |

These are **descriptive evidence categories, not performance scores**.

A documented factor does not establish that the factor caused the outcome.

