# 4. Recovery-Based Delistings

## 4.1 Cumberland sandwort

### Starting condition

Cumberland sandwort was listed because of its highly restricted distribution and vulnerability to habitat disturbance.

### Institutional response

The conservation response involved the Fish and Wildlife Service, Tennessee agencies, the U.S. Forest Service, recreation managers, and other partners.

### Evidence of intervention

The available Service evidence documents a substantial increase in known occurrences. When the recovery plan was completed, approximately 28 sites were known. Later surveys identified approximately 71 known sites.

The Service also documented numerous self-sustaining occurrences meeting recovery criteria, with many occurring on federal or state conservation lands.

Management included monitoring, recreation-related mitigation, reintroduction work, and seed-bank activities.

### Preliminary coding

| Factor                          | Assessment            |
| ------------------------------- | --------------------- |
| Early detection/intervention    | Partially documented  |
| Habitat protection              | Documented            |
| Threat reduction                | Documented            |
| Active management               | Documented            |
| Monitoring                      | Documented            |
| Recovery-plan implementation    | Documented            |
| Cross-jurisdiction coordination | Documented            |
| Private-land participation      | Insufficient evidence |
| Long-term funding/capacity      | Insufficient evidence |
| Adaptive management             | Partially documented  |

### Institutional lesson

This case suggests that recovery can occur when formal ESA protection is combined with site-level habitat protection, monitoring, recreation management, and coordinated implementation across land-management jurisdictions.

The ESA listing itself does not explain the outcome. The recovery appears to have depended on a broader institutional network.

