## 4.2 San Benito evening-primrose

### Starting condition

The species occupied a limited number of locations when listed and faced substantial habitat disturbance, particularly from motorized recreation.

### Institutional response

The conservation response included the Fish and Wildlife Service, Bureau of Land Management, local land managers, and conservation partners.

### Evidence of intervention

The Service documented expansion from approximately nine known locations at listing to more than 100 areas across multiple watersheds.

Management measures included restrictions incorporated into BLM land-management planning and measures addressing motorized recreation.

Post-delisting monitoring was incorporated into the recovery process.

### Preliminary coding

| Factor                          | Assessment            |
| ------------------------------- | --------------------- |
| Early detection/intervention    | Partially documented  |
| Habitat protection              | Documented            |
| Threat reduction                | Documented            |
| Active management               | Partially documented  |
| Monitoring                      | Documented            |
| Recovery-plan implementation    | Documented            |
| Cross-jurisdiction coordination | Partially documented  |
| Private-land participation      | Insufficient evidence |
| Long-term funding/capacity      | Insufficient evidence |
| Adaptive management             | Partially documented  |

### Institutional lesson

The case illustrates the importance of incorporating species conservation into broader land-management decisions.

A species recovery program does not necessarily require a single dedicated conservation institution. It can also depend on modifying the activities of institutions whose primary responsibilities are broader than species conservation.

---

## 4.3 Running buffalo clover

### Starting condition

Running buffalo clover experienced substantial historical decline and habitat loss.

### Institutional response

Conservation involved federal, state, local, and private landowners, along with conservation agreements and monitoring by government agencies and members of the public.

### Evidence of intervention

The Service documented approximately 175 populations, with many occurring on public or privately managed lands subject to conservation agreements.

Management activities included protection and restoration of habitat, enforcement of restrictions on off-road vehicle use, national-forest management, monitoring, and responses to threats affecting forest canopy.

Amateur botanists also contributed observations.

### Preliminary coding

| Factor                          | Assessment           |
| ------------------------------- | -------------------- |
| Early detection/intervention    | Partially documented |
| Habitat protection              | Documented           |
| Threat reduction                | Documented           |
| Active management               | Documented           |
| Monitoring                      | Documented           |
| Recovery-plan implementation    | Documented           |
| Cross-jurisdiction coordination | Documented           |
| Private-land participation      | Documented           |
| Long-term funding/capacity      | Partially documented |
| Adaptive management             | Partially documented |

### Institutional lesson

The case is particularly useful for examining **distributed conservation capacity**.

Recovery did not depend exclusively on federal land ownership. It involved conservation occurring across multiple ownership categories and relied partly on networks of landowners, agencies, and observers.

