## 4.4 Interior least tern

### Starting condition

The interior least tern experienced major historical declines associated with changes to river systems and nesting habitat.

### Institutional response

The conservation response involved the Fish and Wildlife Service, Army Corps of Engineers, state agencies, industries, local partners, and land managers.

### Evidence of intervention

The Service documented an increase from fewer than 2,000 birds at listing to more than 18,000 birds occupying more than 480 sites.

Conservation measures included monitoring, nesting protection, management of water flows, vegetation and predator management, and the use of dredged material to create or maintain nesting habitat.

### Preliminary coding

| Factor                          | Assessment           |
| ------------------------------- | -------------------- |
| Early detection/intervention    | Partially documented |
| Habitat protection              | Documented           |
| Threat reduction                | Documented           |
| Active management               | Documented           |
| Monitoring                      | Documented           |
| Recovery-plan implementation    | Documented           |
| Cross-jurisdiction coordination | Documented           |
| Private-land participation      | Partially documented |
| Long-term funding/capacity      | Documented           |
| Adaptive management             | Documented           |

### Institutional lesson

The interior least tern is a strong example of conservation operating at the scale of an ecological system rather than a single property.

Its recovery required institutions managing rivers, navigation, habitat, water flows, and wildlife to interact.

