# 10. Methodological Guardrails

The case-study analysis should maintain several safeguards.

### Do not equate correlation with causation

If a species recovered after habitat protection, habitat protection should not automatically be identified as the sole cause.

### Do not treat FWS attribution as independent causal evidence

When the Service identifies a management action as contributing to recovery, that is important evidence, but it remains an institutional assessment rather than an independent causal estimate.

### Do not equate legal status with ecological condition

A species can improve substantially without meeting the legal criteria for downlisting.

Conversely, a species can remain listed while experiencing meaningful improvement.

### Do not equate continued listing with conservation failure

Some species may require prolonged management because their ecological recovery is inherently slow or because residual threats cannot yet be eliminated.

### Do not equate delisting with permanent success

Post-delisting monitoring exists precisely because conservation gains can be lost.

### Do not assume every deterioration case reflects poor management

Some threats may be extremely difficult to control or may emerge faster than institutions can respond.

