# 11. Next Research Step

The next phase should move from qualitative case selection to **structured evidence extraction**.

For each of the 24 cases, the research dataset should record:

* species
* taxonomic group
* listing date
* initial listing status
* recovery priority
* principal threats
* habitat condition
* recovery-plan date
* recovery criteria
* major recovery actions
* responsible institutions
* state participation
* Tribal participation
* private-land participation
* funding mechanisms
* monitoring programs
* population trend
* distribution/range trend
* habitat trend
* threat trend
* status-review recommendation
* final legal outcome
* post-delisting monitoring, where applicable
* evidence of adaptive management
* evidence quality
* documented contributing factors
* unresolved uncertainties

The resulting dataset will allow the project to move beyond individual narratives and identify recurring **institutional configurations associated with different conservation trajectories**.

The eventual objective is not to identify a universal recipe for recovery.

It is to determine which parts of the conservation system appear to work reliably, which appear contingent on particular ecological circumstances, and where institutional redesign could plausibly improve outcomes.

---

## Working conclusion

The evidence examined so far points toward a more useful conception of wildlife-conservation performance.

The central question is not simply whether the United States has enough conservation laws, agencies, programs, or protected lands.

It is whether those components form a system capable of:

**Detecting decline → diagnosing causes → mobilizing intervention → coordinating across boundaries → measuring biological response → adapting management → achieving recovery → maintaining gains.**

That sequence will serve as the working performance model for the remainder of the Wildlife & Biodiversity pillar.
